Subject: fixed.x=FALSE or TRUE in multiple regression?
Message-ID: <96gsyGeEaV8.wifTM73xuvcJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/96gsyGeEaV8/m/wifTM73xuvcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

I run several multiple regressions with lavaan, where some models are nested by putting constraints on the paths. I need the intercepts, so I set meanstructure to TRUE. Now I am not sure about the fixed.x option. I read the help page for it, but I am not sure what would be the correct way of doing it.

When no constraints are present, results are identical (fixed.x = TRUE or FALSE), but as soon as one path is constrained to be 0, or other equality constraints are set, results can diverge a lot.

Can someone clarify this option, or direct me to a paper where I can read about the difference? What would be the correct option for plain multiple regressions (1 DV, with constraints)?

Here's a minimal example:

library(lavaan)
set.seed(0xBEEF)
x <- rnorm(100)
y <- rnorm(100)
z <- 0.4*x + 0.7*y + rnorm(100, 0, 0.4)
df <- data.frame(x, y, z)

m1 <- "z ~ b1*x + b2*y"
m2 <- "z ~ b1*x + 0*y"
m3 <- "z ~ b1*x + b2*y
b1==b2"

s1 <- sem(m1, data=df, meanstructure=TRUE)
summary(s1)

s2 <- sem(m1, data=df, meanstructure=TRUE, fixed.x=FALSE)
summary(s2)

# m1 gives identical results for s1 and s2, m2 and m3 gives different results

Thanks,
Felix

