On 11/26/2012 05:26 PM, Robert Miller wrote:
> Hello everyone,
>
> very recently i encountered a problem, which was not present in lavaan v.4.

could you be more specific: which version of lavaan v4? (I tried 0.4-10
and 0.4-14, but got the same results as 0.5-10).

> Specifically, i tried model a change score regression. However,
> predicted change scores deviate from the "true" change scores:
>
> /library(lavaan)
>
> *#generate data*
> x <- rnorm(150,5,1)
> change <- rnorm(150,2,1)
> y <- x + change
> df <- data.frame(x,y,change)
>
> *#specify model*
> mod_lc <- "
> #latent change regression
> change =~ 1*y
> change ~ x
> #mean structure
> x ~ 1
> y ~ 0
> change ~ 1
> #variances
> x ~~ x
> change ~~ change
> "
> *
> #fit model and compare change scores*
> fit_lc <- lavaan(mod_lc, orthogonal=T, fixed.x=F, auto.fix.single = T,
> data=df[,1:2])
> summary(fit_lc, fit.measures=T, standardized=T, rsquare=T)
> head(round(cbind(predict(fit_lc), df[,3]),3))/

Hm. As you specify your model, 'change' is set equal to 'y'. So, AFAICS,
predict() will give scores that are equal to 'change + x', but not 'change':

fit.lm <- lm(y ~ x, data=df)
head(cbind(predict(fit_lc), df$x + df$change))

> To my mind, this is likely to be due to the fixed.x - argument, which
> somehow causes lavaan v.5 (in contrast to lavaan v.4) to estimate mean
> and variance of /x/ instead of fixing it to the sample value. Does
> anyone has an idea how to solve this problem?

As far as I know, the behavior of the fixed.x argument has not changed
between v4 and v5. If fixed.x=TRUE (and you remove both x~1 and x~~x
from your syntax!), the mean/variance of x is fixed at its sample value.
But this does not affect the predict() values.

Yves.

